-- Hold Down Helper help page. Loaded on demand by OnLuaButton_InquiryHelp in Hold_Down_Helper.lua, the same way
-- Blum Drawer Maker loads its Help/*.xlua pages, and shown in an HTML_Dialog. ButtonOK is the dialog's Close button.
]] -- =====================================================]]
function HoldDownHelpHtml()
    return [==[<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><title>Hold Down Helper Help</title>
<style>
body { font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 13px; background-color: #FFFFFF; color: #000000; margin: 0; }
.layout { overflow: auto; height: 540px; padding: 4px 14px 4px 10px; }
h1 { font-size: 20px; margin: 10px 0 4px 0; }
h2 { font-size: 16px; margin: 16px 0 4px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #999999; }
p, li { line-height: 1.35; }
table { border-collapse: collapse; margin: 4px 0; }
th, td { border: 1px solid #BBBBBB; padding: 3px 6px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top; }
th { background-color: #EEEEEE; }
.warn { background-color: #FFF4CE; border: 1px solid #D9A400; padding: 6px 8px; margin: 8px 0; }
.danger { background-color: #FDE2E1; border: 1px solid #C0392B; padding: 6px 8px; margin: 8px 0; }
.foot { text-align: center; padding: 8px; border-top: 1px solid #999999; }
.FormButton { font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; }
</style></head>
<body>
<div class="layout">

<h1>Hold Down Helper</h1>
<p>Hold Down Helper finds safe screw positions on the <b>active sheet</b>. At a safe position, no cutter touches
the screw during the job.</p>
<p>The gadget does these tasks:</p>
<ul>
<li>It draws a small circle (a marker) at each safe position, on the <b>Hold Down</b> layer.</li>
<li>It makes one drilling toolpath, <b>Hold Down Countersinks</b>. This toolpath cuts a countersink at each marker
with a V-bit.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each countersink is a little wider than the screw head. Thus, the top of the screw head is below the surface of
the sheet.</p>

<h2>Procedure</h2>
<ol>
<li>Draw and nest the job.</li>
<li>Make the sheet that you will cut next the active sheet.</li>
<li>Run Hold Down Helper.</li>
<li>Put the sheet on the spoilboard.</li>
<li>Set the X and Y zero.</li>
<li>Run <b>only</b> the <b>Hold Down Countersinks</b> toolpath. Use the V-bit that you selected in the gadget.</li>
<li>Put a screw in each countersink.</li>
<li>Run the job toolpaths.</li>
</ol>
<div class="danger"><b>WARNING:</b> Do not set the X or Y zero again after step 5. The countersinks are in job
coordinates. If you change the zero, all cuts move, but the screws do not move. A cutter can then hit a screw.</div>

<h2>Before you start</h2>
<ul>
<li>Make sure that the tool database has a <b>V-bit</b>. The units of the V-bit must be the <b>same as the job
units</b>. If the units are different, the gadget shows an error.</li>
<li>Show all layers that the job cuts. The gadget <b>does not examine hidden layers</b>. Hide a layer when you
want the gadget to ignore it, for example a layer with part labels or construction lines. The message at the end
shows the names of the hidden layers. Make sure that you did not hide a layer with parts on it.</li>
<li>Do not draw on the <b>Hold Down</b> layer. Each run deletes all objects on this layer on the active
sheet.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Settings</h2>
<p>All values are in the job units. The gadget keeps the values for the next run. It keeps one set of values for
inch jobs and a different set for metric jobs.</p>
<div class="warn"><b>CAUTION:</b> Type each value, then push the Tab key. VCarve does not keep a value that you
paste.</div>
<table>
<tr><th>Setting</th><th>Inch default</th><th>mm default</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>V-Bit</td><td colspan="2">none</td><td>The bit that cuts the countersinks. The tool list shows only
V-bits. The angle of the bit sets the depth of the countersinks. The diameter of the bit must be equal to or more
than the countersink diameter. The gadget keeps a copy of the bit data. If you change the bit in the tool
database, select the bit again.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assumed tool diameter</td><td>0.25</td><td>6.0</td><td>The diameter of the <b>largest</b> cutter in the
job. The gadget examines all vectors as if this cutter cuts each of them.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Screw head diameter</td><td>0.25</td><td>6.0</td><td>The diameter of the screw head.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Margin</td><td>0.125</td><td>3.0</td><td>An additional distance between the cutter and the screw
head.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Edge inset</td><td>1.25</td><td>32.0</td><td>The distance from the sheet edge to the perimeter
screws.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Perimeter spacing target</td><td>16.0</td><td>406.4</td><td>The approximate distance between the screws
along an edge. Each edge gets a minimum of 2 screws. For example, a 48" edge gets 3 screws.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Field count</td><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>The number of screws in the center area of the sheet. The maximum
is 36. Type 0 to put screws only along the edges.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Max nudge search</td><td>3.0</td><td>76.2</td><td>The maximum distance that the gadget can move a
position that is not safe.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Countersink clearance</td><td>0.02</td><td>0.5</td><td>The countersink diameter minus the screw head
diameter. The gadget calculates the depth from the countersink diameter and the angle of the V-bit:<br>
depth = (countersink diameter / 2) / tan(V-bit angle / 2)<br>
For example, a 0.25" screw head and a 90 degree V-bit give a countersink 0.27" wide and 0.135" deep. The depth must
be less than the sheet thickness.<br>
<b>NOTE:</b> Most US wood screws have an 82 degree head. In a countersink cut by a 90 degree V-bit, only the outer
edge of the head touches the sheet. This is satisfactory for a hold-down screw.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Marker diameter</td><td>0.125</td><td>3.0</td><td>The diameter of the circle at each position. The drilling
toolpath needs a vector at each position. The V-bit drills at the center of the circle. This value has no effect
on the countersink.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Safe Z gap</td><td>0.25</td><td>6.0</td><td>The height of the bit above the sheet when the bit moves from
one countersink to the next. The maximum is 1" (25.4 mm). The screws are not in the sheet yet, so the sheet can
bend or move. If the bit touches the sheet during these moves, increase this value.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>How the gadget finds a safe position</h2>
<p>The gadget rejects a position if one of these conditions is true:</p>
<ul>
<li>The position is <b>inside</b> a closed vector. Thus, a screw never goes through a part.</li>
<li>The distance from the position to a vector is less than <b>R</b>.</li>
<li>The distance from the position to the sheet edge is less than half of the screw head diameter.</li>
</ul>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;"><b>R = assumed tool diameter + (screw head diameter / 2) + margin</b><br>
With the inch defaults, R is 0.5".</p>
<p>R uses the <b>full</b> tool diameter, not the radius. An outside profile moves the cutter beside the vector.
Thus, the far edge of the cutter is one full diameter from the vector.</p>

<h2>Where the gadget puts the screws</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Perimeter screws:</b> The gadget divides each edge into equal lengths. It puts a screw at the center of each
length, at the edge inset distance from the edge. It does not put a screw in a corner.</li>
<li><b>Field screws:</b> The gadget puts these screws in a grid in the center area of the sheet. The center area
is half of the sheet width and half of the sheet length. For example, 4 screws make a 2 x 2 grid, and 6 screws make
a 2 x 3 grid.</li>
<li>If a perimeter position is not safe, the gadget moves it along its edge. It moves the position in steps of
R / 2, to one side and then to the other side. It <b>does not move the position toward the center</b> of the sheet,
because a screw away from the edge does not hold the edge down.</li>
<li>If a field position is not safe, the gadget moves it outward in rings until the position is safe.</li>
<li>If the gadget cannot find a safe position within the max nudge search distance, it <b>does not put a screw
there</b>. The message at the end shows that position.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The message at the end</h2>
<ul>
<li>The message shows the value of <b>R</b>. If the gadget rejects more positions than you expect, examine R
first. The gadget keeps the values from the last run. A large test value, for example a large margin, decreases
the area for screws.</li>
<li>For each position that the gadget rejects, the message shows the <b>layer and the extents of the vector</b>
that is too near. Or, it shows "too close to the sheet edge".</li>
<li>A <b>NOTE</b> shows the objects that have no vector outline, for example text that is not converted to curves.
The gadget keeps the positions away from the bounding box of each of these objects. An object marked <b>NOT
TESTED</b> has no bounding box, so the gadget ignores it. Examine these areas yourself.</li>
<li>If the gadget rejects all positions, it does not draw or delete objects. The markers and the toolpath from the
last run stay in the job, and they can be unsafe. Delete them before you cut.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Run the gadget again</h2>
<p>When the gadget finds one or more safe positions, it deletes the markers and the Hold Down Countersinks toolpath
on the active sheet. Then it makes new markers and a new toolpath. Thus, new settings replace the old
countersinks.</p>
<p>The gadget does not change other sheets. If you click Cancel, the gadget makes no changes.</p>
<p>Countersinks that you cut stay in the sheet. Put screws only in the countersinks from the last run.</p>
<p>Earlier versions of the gadget made a toolpath with the name Hold Down Dimples. The gadget also deletes this
toolpath on the active sheet.</p>

<h2>Limits</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Edge inset and parts near the edge.</b> If parts are near the sheet edge, the perimeter positions are near
the parts. Then the gadget rejects all positions along that edge. Decrease the edge inset to put the screws between
the parts and the sheet edge. The distance from the sheet edge to the parts must be more than the edge inset +
R.</li>
<li><b>The gadget does not find loose waste.</b> A position can be far from all vectors but on a piece of waste
that the job cuts free.</li>
</ul>
<div class="danger"><b>WARNING:</b> Examine all positions before you cut the countersinks. A screw in a piece that
the job cuts free does not hold the sheet. The loose piece can turn around the screw and hit the cutter. The cutter
can throw the piece.</div>
<ul>
<li><b>One tool diameter for all of the sheet.</b> If a job uses a 1/8" bit and a 1/4" bit, the gadget examines
all vectors as if the 1/4" bit cuts them. This decreases the area for screws, but it is always safe.</li>
<li><b>The gadget does not read toolpaths.</b> It examines only vectors. A vector without a toolpath blocks a
position. A toolpath whose vector you deleted does not block a position.</li>
<li><b>The gadget does not include toolpath extras.</b> Lead-ins, ramps, overcuts and machining allowances can go
farther than R. Increase the margin to include them.</li>
<li><b>Active sheet only.</b> Run the gadget one time for each sheet, before you cut that sheet.</li>
<li><b>Small sheets.</b> The gadget is for full sheets. If the sheet is smaller than 24 x 24 inches (610 x 610 mm),
the gadget shows a warning and then continues.</li>
</ul>

<h2>About</h2>
<p><b>Hold Down Helper</b>, version ]==] .. HoldDown.ProgramVersion .. [==[<br>
An original gadget from the vectric-gadgets repository:
<a href="https://github.com/tippmar/vectric-gadgets">https://github.com/tippmar/vectric-gadgets</a><br>
Questions and discussion: <a href="https://forum.vectric.com">Vectric User Forum</a></p>
<p><b>Disclaimer</b><br>
The Hold Down Helper gadget is a plugin for Vectric software, VCarve Pro and Aspire.
Gadgets are an entirely optional add-in to Vectric's core software products.
They are provided 'as-is', without any express or implied warranty, and you make use of them entirely at your own
risk. In no event will the author(s) or Vectric Ltd. be held liable for any damages arising from their use.</p>
<p>Permission is granted to anyone to use this software for any purpose, including commercial applications, and to
alter it and redistribute it freely, subject to the following restrictions:</p>
<ol>
<li>The origin of this software must not be misrepresented; you must not claim that you wrote the original
software. If you use this software in a product, an acknowledgement in the product documentation would be
appreciated but is not required.</li>
<li>Altered source versions must be plainly marked as such, and must not be misrepresented as being the original
software.</li>
<li>This notice may not be removed or altered from any source distribution.</li>
</ol>

</div>
<div class="foot">
<input id="ButtonOK" class="FormButton" name="ButtonOK" type="button" value="Close">
</div>
</body></html>]==]
end
-- =============== End of File =========================]]
